<form action="/exercises" method="post" enctype="multipart/form-data">
  <%== csrf_tag('/exercises') %>
  <div>
    <label for="name" class="block text-sm font-medium leading-6 text-gray-900">Name</label>
    <div class="mt-2">
      <input value="<%= @exercise&.name || nil %>" name="name" id="name"
             class="<%= field_style %> block w-full py-1.5 text-gray-900 placeholder:text-gray-400 sm:text-sm sm:leading-6">
    </div>
    <%# Coaching intent for the movement itself: why it is in the program, what to
        watch for, the cue that makes it work. It belongs here rather than on a set
        because "this helps correct valgus" is true of the lift every time it is done,
        not of one Tuesday's third set. text-base is what the browser was already giving
        the inputs above by way of Tailwind's preflight, written out because a textarea
        under 16px is the size iOS Safari zooms the page in on when it takes focus, and
        the way back out is a pinch. min-h-11 is the 44px a thumb is aimed at; rows="3"
        already clears it, and the floor is here so shrinking the row count later cannot
        quietly take the tap target with it. Everything between the two tags is the
        field's value, newlines and indentation included, so the note has to butt
        straight up against both -- which is why the long class list is not the last
        attribute here as it is on the inputs above. %>
    <label for="note" class="mt-4 block text-sm font-medium leading-6 text-gray-900">Note</label>
    <div class="mt-2">
      <textarea name="note" id="note" rows="3"
                       class="<%= field_style %> block min-h-11 w-full py-1.5 text-base text-gray-900 placeholder:text-gray-400 sm:text-sm sm:leading-6"
                placeholder="Why it is in the program, and what to watch for."><%= @exercise&.note %></textarea>
    </div>
    <%# The one thing about a movement the app cannot work out from its name once the
        movement is not one of the built-in fifty-four: whether its sets are loaded on a
        bar, and so whether they get a plate breakdown and a warmup ramp. %>
    <div class="mt-4 flex items-center gap-2">
      <input type="checkbox" name="is_barbell" id="is_barbell" value="1" <%= 'checked' if @exercise&.is_barbell %> class="h-4 w-4 rounded border-gray-300 text-lime-500 accent-lime-500 focus:ring-lime-500">
      <label for="is_barbell" class="text-sm font-medium leading-6 text-gray-900">Loaded on a barbell</label>
    </div>
    <%# The second thing the app cannot work out from a name, and the one that changes the
        arithmetic rather than the drawing: a movement done one limb at a time is written
        3x8 and lifted sixteen times, so counted_reps doubles it and every volume figure
        downstream depends on the answer. #279.

        The column has existed since the per-side work and every write path could set it --
        create_program, add_program_lift, the MCP exercise tools -- except the one a person
        actually uses. So a Bulgarian split squat added in the browser was half the volume
        it should have been, silently, and the only way to correct it was to ask an
        assistant to do it.

        A default rather than a fact about one set: a prescription may override it for a
        block that wants the dumbbell press done one arm at a time, which is what
        shape_of already reads. This is what that override falls back to. %>
    <div class="mt-4 flex items-center gap-2">
      <input type="checkbox" name="default_is_per_side" id="default_is_per_side" value="1"
             <%= 'checked' if @exercise&.default_is_per_side %>
             class="h-4 w-4 rounded border-gray-300 text-lime-500 accent-lime-500 focus:ring-lime-500">
      <label for="default_is_per_side" class="text-sm font-medium leading-6 text-gray-900">Counted per side</label>
    </div>
    <%# The third thing the app cannot work out from a name, and the one that decides which
        weights exist. #439.

        Plates go on both ends of every handle in use, so one dumbbell spends a pair of a size
        and two spend two pairs. On a 4 lb handle with pairs of 10, 5 and 2.5 that is the
        difference between reaching 79 and reaching 39 -- a single-arm row at 44 is ordinary
        and a dumbbell bench at 44 cannot be built at all. Both used to round against the
        single's list.

        Three options rather than a checkbox, because "not said" is a real answer and has to
        stay sayable. The app reads it as two, which is the cautious reading rather than the
        likely one -- every weight loadable on a pair is loadable on a single, so an
        unanswered movement comes out a little light instead of impossible -- but a schema
        default would make it indistinguishable from a deliberate two, and then nothing could
        tell a lifter which of their movements still wants an answer.

        Shown whatever the movement is, rather than hidden behind the barbell checkbox: the
        two controls are independent, and a field that appears and disappears as another is
        ticked is worse to use than one line of text that does not apply. The label says which
        movements it is about. %>
    <%# The rest this movement is taken with, and the one thing on this form that makes a
        noise. #456.

        The session screen has been able to count a rest down and ring since #281 and #395,
        and it has never done it for anybody: it rings on a rest the programme prescribes, and
        nothing had ever prescribed one -- 126 program lifts, not one rest. The block editor
        that could have set them was removed in #411, so the only way to ask for a bell was to
        ask an assistant, lift by lift.

        Named by the lifter here, which is what earns the bell. A countdown that rings unasked
        is the app interrupting a session, and #263 settled that the app does not decide how
        long anybody rests -- so a number the app picked could not ring, and this one can,
        because somebody typed it.

        Blank is the ordinary answer and stays the default: most movements want no bell, and
        the timer goes on offering the lifter's own median with a tap, exactly as before. %>
    <div class="mt-4">
      <label for="default_rest_seconds" class="block text-sm font-medium leading-6 text-gray-900">
        Rest between sets
      </label>
      <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-500">
        The session screen counts this down after you tick a set off, and rings when it is up.
        Leave it blank for no bell &mdash; the timer still offers however long you usually take.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-2 flex items-center gap-2">
        <%# @rest rather than a column on the movement since 039: a rest is a fact about the
            lifter and the movement together, keyed on the pair the way the training max is, so
            that a library Back Squat can carry one per account instead of none at all. %>
        <input type="number" id="default_rest_seconds" name="default_rest_seconds"
               min="5" max="1800" step="5" autocomplete="off"
               value="<%= @rest %>"
               class="<%= field_style %> w-28 py-1.5 text-gray-900">
        <span class="text-sm text-gray-500">seconds</span>
      </div>
      <%# Said back in the units a person thinks in, through the same phrasing the session
          screen and the turnaround readings use -- "180 seconds" and "3m" are the same fact,
          and two spellings of it in one app is one too many. %>
      <%- if @rest %>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-500">
          That is
          <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900"><%= Tectonic::Timing.phrase(@rest) %></span>
          between sets.
        </p>
      <%- end %>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-4">
      <label for="dumbbell_count" class="block text-sm font-medium leading-6 text-gray-900">Dumbbells</label>
      <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-500">
        How many are in your hands at once, for movements not loaded on a bar. Two dumbbells
        need each plate size twice over, so fewer weights are possible than with one.
      </p>
      <select name="dumbbell_count" id="dumbbell_count"
              class="<%= field_style %> mt-2 min-h-11 w-full px-2 text-base text-gray-900">
        <%- [['', 'Not said'], ['1', 'One'], ['2', 'Two']].each do |value, label| %>
          <option value="<%= value %>" <%= 'selected' if @exercise&.dumbbell_count.to_s == value %>>
            <%= label %>
          </option>
        <%- end %>
      </select>
    </div>
  </div>
  <input type="hidden" name="id" id="id" value="<%= @exercise&.id || nil %>">
  <input type="submit" value="Save" class="<%= button_style %> mt-4 block bg-lime-500 px-3 py-2 text-center text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 hover:bg-lime-600">
</form>
